## Lane Guide — backend

### Tools
- tests: Vitest for unit and service tests, Supertest for Express route verification, seeded SQLite fixtures for integration tests
- lint: ESLint with import and unused-variable checks, plus consistent script-level formatting rules
- types: if TypeScript is introduced, use `tsc --noEmit`; if JavaScript stays primary, keep runtime schema validation and JSDoc-checked contracts for shared boundaries
- security: `npm audit` with zero critical vulnerabilities, dependency review for AI and SQLite packages, regression tests for path handling and input validation
- build: `npm run build` must package the server and verify production-start compatibility for Node 18+

### CLI Examples
- Local: `npm ci && npm run test:backend && npm run lint && npm run build`
- Containerized: `docker run --rm -v "$PWD:/workspace" -w /workspace node:18 bash -lc "npm ci && npm run test:backend && npm run lint && npm run build"`

### Default Gate Policy
- min coverage: 70 percent
- max criticals: 0
- required checks: tests, lint, build, runtime-profile adherence
- additional checks for AI-backed slices: structured-output validation tests, redaction checks, no-side-effect failure-path tests

### Enterprise Runner Notes
- SonarQube: not configured in MVP; if introduced later, point it at backend sources and tests without changing local command semantics
- Jenkins: not configured in MVP; manual-local execution remains the baseline, and any future runner should call the same npm scripts used locally

### TECH_CONSTRAINTS integration
- air-gap: not required by current constraints; outbound HTTPS must remain restricted to the configured AI provider endpoint only
- registries: use the standard npm registry model unless an internal mirror is later mandated; do not hardcode registry assumptions into feature code
- runtime: Node.js 18+ and Express-compatible server behavior are required
- storage: backend tests should use SQLite temp files and local filesystem fixtures, not external managed services
- auth: no user auth in MVP, so denial coverage should focus on business-precondition failures and invalid state transitions
- deployment parity: the same backend code and env keys must run on a single local workstation and an on-prem single VM
